You can create a class `Animal` with properties for name and lifespan, and then create an array or list of `Animal` objects with the respective values. Then, you can use the `Comparator` interface to sort the list based on the lifespan property. Here's an example Java code:
```
import java.util.*;
class Animal {
    String name;
    int lifespan;

    public Animal(String name, int lifespan) {
        this.name = name;
        this.lifespan = lifespan;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getLifespan() {
        return lifespan;
    }
}
public class AnimalSorter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal[] animals = {
            new Animal("Frog", 10),
            new Animal("Elephant", 70),
            new Animal("Giraffe", 25),
            new Animal("Cat", 15)
        };

        Arrays.sort(animals, Comparator.comparingInt(Animal::getLifespan));

        for (Animal animal : animals) {
            System.out.println(animal.getName() + " (" + animal.getLifespan() + " years)");
        }
    }
}
```
This will output:
```
Frog (10 years)
Cat (15 years)
Giraffe (25 years)
Elephant (70 years)
```
As you can see, the animals are sorted in ascending order based on their lifespan.

