To navigate through the maze, you can use a search algorithm like Breadth-First Search (BFS) or Depth-First Search (DFS). These algorithms will allow you to explore the maze and find the treasure while avoiding obstacles and locked doors.
Here is an example implementation of BFS:
```
import java.util.*;
public class Maze {
  int[][] maze; //2D array representing maze
  int[] startPos; //starting position as [x, y]
  int[] treasurePos; //treasure position as [x, y]

  //constructor to initialize maze and positions
  public Maze(int[][] maze, int[] startPos, int[] treasurePos) {
    this.maze = maze;
    this.startPos = startPos;
    this.treasurePos = treasurePos;
  }

  //method to navigate through maze and find treasure
  public void navigateMaze() {
    Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>(); //queue for BFS
    Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>(); //set to keep track of visited nodes
    Map<String, String> parent = new HashMap<>(); //map to keep track of parent nodes

    //add starting position to queue and visited set
    queue.add(startPos);
    visited.add(Arrays.toString(startPos));

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      int[] curr = queue.poll();

      //if current position is treasure, print path and return
      if (Arrays.equals(curr, treasurePos)) {
        printPath(parent, curr);
        return;
      }

      //explore neighboring nodes
      for (int[] neighbor : getNeighbors(curr)) {
        //if neighbor is not visited and not an obstacle, add to queue and visited set
        if (!visited.contains(Arrays.toString(neighbor)) && maze[neighbor[0]][neighbor[1]] != -1) {
          queue.add(neighbor);
          visited.add(Arrays.toString(neighbor));
          parent.put(Arrays.toString(neighbor), Arrays.toString(curr));
        }
      }
    }

    System.out.println("Treasure not found.");
  }

  //helper method to get neighboring nodes
  private List<int[]> getNeighbors(int[] curr) {
    List<int[]> neighbors = new ArrayList<>();
    int x = curr[0];
    int y = curr[1];

    //check up
    if (x > 0) {
      neighbors.add(new int[] {x - 1, y});
    }

    //check down
    if (x < maze.length - 1) {
      neighbors.add(new int[] {x + 1, y});
    }

    //check left
    if (y > 0) {
      neighbors.add(new int[] {x, y - 1});
    }

    //check right
    if (y < maze[0].length - 1) {
      neighbors.add(new int[] {x, y + 1});
    }

    return neighbors;
  }

  //helper method to print path from starting position to treasure
  private void printPath(Map<String, String> parent, int[] curr) {
    List<int[]> path = new ArrayList<>();
    path.add(curr);

    while (parent.containsKey(Arrays.toString(curr))) {
      curr = stringToIntArray(parent.get(Arrays.toString(curr)));
      path.add(0, curr);
    }

    System.out.println("Path from starting position to treasure:");
    for (int[] pos : path) {
      System.out.println(Arrays.toString(pos));
    }
  }

  //helper method to convert string to int array
  private int[] stringToIntArray(String s) {
    s = s.substring(1, s.length() - 1);
    String[] arr = s.split(", ");
    int[] result = new int[arr.length];
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
      result[i] = Integer.parseInt(arr[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```
In this implementation, we use a BFS algorithm to explore the maze and find the treasure. We keep track of visited nodes using a set, and we keep track of parent nodes using a map. We start at the starting position and add it to the queue and visited set. We then loop through the queue, exploring neighboring nodes and adding them to the queue and visited set if they are not obstacles and have not been visited before. We also keep track of parent nodes so that we can print the path from the starting position to the treasure once we find it.
The `getNeighbors` method returns a list of neighboring nodes for a given position. We check up, down, left, and right neighbors and return them if they are within the bounds of the maze.
The `printPath` method takes in the parent map and the current position and prints the path from the starting position to the treasure. We use the parent map to backtrack from the treasure to the starting position and add each position to a list. We then print the list in reverse order to get the path from the starting position to the treasure.
Note that this implementation assumes that obstacles are represented by -1 in the maze array. You may need to adjust this if your maze uses a different representation for obstacles.

