To find the longest continuous increasing subarray, we can use a sliding window approach. We start with two pointers, left and right, pointing to the beginning of the array. We also keep track of the current length of the increasing subarray and the maximum length seen so far. Then we move the right pointer to the right until we find a decreasing element, at which point we update the maximum length if necessary. Finally, we move the left pointer to the right until we find the next increasing element, and repeat the process until we reach the end of the array.
To optimize the algorithm, we can use a binary search to find the next increasing element when we move the left pointer. This reduces the time complexity from O(n^2) to O(n log n).
To find the second longest increasing subarray, we can modify the algorithm slightly. Instead of updating the maximum length when we find a decreasing element, we update the second longest length if necessary. We also keep track of the starting and ending indices of the longest and second longest subarrays.
Here is the C# implementation:
```
public static void FindLongestIncreasingSubarray(int[] myArray, out int maxLength, out int start, out int end)
{
    maxLength = 1;
    start = end = 0;
    int left = 0;
    int right = 1;
    while (right < myArray.Length)
    {
        if (myArray[right] <= myArray[right - 1])
        {
            if (right - left > maxLength)
            {
                maxLength = right - left;
                start = left;
                end = right - 1;
            }
            left = right;
        }
        right++;
    }
    if (right - left > maxLength)
    {
        maxLength = right - left;
        start = left;
        end = right - 1;
    }
}
public static void FindSecondLongestIncreasingSubarray(int[] myArray, out int maxLength, out int start1, out int end1, out int start2, out int end2)
{
    maxLength = start1 = end1 = start2 = end2 = 0;
    int left = 0;
    int right = 1;
    while (right < myArray.Length)
    {
        if (myArray[right] <= myArray[right - 1])
        {
            int length = right - left;
            if (length > maxLength)
            {
                maxLength = length;
                start2 = start1;
                end2 = end1;
                start1 = left;
                end1 = right - 1;
            }
            else if (length > maxLength - 1)
            {
                start2 = left;
                end2 = right - 1;
            }
            left = right;
        }
        right++;
    }
    int lastLength = right - left;
    if (lastLength > maxLength)
    {
        maxLength = lastLength;
        start2 = start1;
        end2 = end1;
        start1 = left;
        end1 = right - 1;
    }
    else if (lastLength > maxLength - 1)
    {
        start2 = left;
        end2 = right - 1;
    }
}
```

