        int[] arr1 = {21, 4, 8, 15, 3, 17};
        int[] arr2 = {5, 15, 10, 21, 7, 4, 9};

        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> indexMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
            if (!indexMap.containsKey(arr1[i])) {
                indexMap.put(arr1[i], new ArrayList<>());
            }
            indexMap.get(arr1[i]).add(i);
        }

        List<Integer[]> matchingValues = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < arr2.length; i++) {
            if (indexMap.containsKey(arr2[i])) {
                for (int j : indexMap.get(arr2[i])) {
                    matchingValues.add(new Integer[]{arr2[i], j, i});
                }
            }
        }

        for (Integer[] match : matchingValues) {
            System.out.println("Matching value: " + match[0] + ", index in arr1: " + match[1] + ", index in arr2: " + match[2]);
        }
    }
}
```
This program first creates a `HashMap` to store the indices of each value in the first array. It then iterates through the second array, checking if each value is in the `HashMap`. If it is, it adds the matching value and the corresponding indices in both arrays to a list. Finally, it prints out the list of matching values and their indices.
Note that this program has an optimized runtime complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the second array. This is because it only needs to iterate through each value in the second array once, and the `HashMap` lookup is an O(1) operation.

